  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This review synthesizes recent research on large language models (LLMs) in intelligent transportation systems, proposing a system-level framework called TrafficMind where LLMs act as a shared semantic and reasoning layer. The analysis spans applications in perception, forecasting, control, planning, and coordination, examining how LLMs integrate with reinforcement learning, knowledge graphs, and digital twins.",
      "why": "It establishes that the primary value of LLMs in transportation is not numerical optimization or real-time actuation, but their capacity to unify heterogeneous data, regulations, and human inputs. It also provides a safety-oriented design principle, showing that LLMs are currently most reliable when kept outside of closed-loop control in safety-critical settings like traffic signals and urban air mobility."
    }
